  "body": "This PR is the lathe slice of #3643. It is pure domain code in `camcraft-tools`. It adds no crate edge, no ECS code and no file I/O.\n\n## What changed\n\n- New `crates/tools/src/lathe_assembly.rs` (with `lathe_assembly_error.rs`). `resolve_lathe` resolves a turning holder or a boring bar, its insert and its adapters to these values:\n  - the nose centre as the tip of the turning frame (a `LathePlacement`, so the current lathe body path reads it without change);\n  - the CRP in the turning frame;\n  - the orientation (LinuxCNC 1-8): derived from the root coupling (rectangular shank = OD, round shank = boring bar), the seat hand and the insert shape (round = neutral), or stated by the caller;\n  - the nose radius, the approach angle, the hand, the insert corner angle, the clearance angle and the inscribed circle;\n  - one `LatheClearance` over the bodies of all components except the insert.\n- The CRP is the insert's cutting point (its CIP, else its seat frame). We define it as the control point of the orientation chart: the point where the nose tangents parallel to X and Z meet. The nose centre is `CRP - r * tip_offset(q)` in the turning frame.\n- `LatheBody` is a set of boxes or a mesh in the frame of the component. A revolved body on a lathe component is refused by name. It is never dropped.\n- `LatheStation` states which root directions are radial and along the spindle (`RADIAL_SHANK`, `AXIAL_SHANK`).\n- `tool_assembly::placed` (new, `pub(crate)`) holds the chain steps that `resolve` had before the gauge length. `resolve` calls it now. The lathe resolver gets the same #3642 fit refusals, port refusals, cycle refusals and frame refusals.\n- `lathe_insert_zone::clip_axis` is now `pub(crate)`. The edge check uses it.\n- Decision: a lathe section in `docs/decisions/3643-tool-assemblies.md`.\n\n## Named refusals\n\n- every refusal of the component chain (for example a CNMG09 in a PCLNR..12 seat: fit property `size`);\n- a cutting item that is not an insert;\n- an insert with no nose radius (a sharp corner or a wiper facet);\n- a stated orientation that is not 1 to 8;\n- a root coupling that does not show OD or ID (VDI, BMT, custom): \"state the orientation\";\n- a bad station (a zero direction, directions that are not square);\n- a revolved body on a lathe component;\n- a holder body past the cutting lines of the insert, within one insert (25.4 mm) of the nose. This catches a wrong hand, a wrong stated orientation or a wrong seat frame. The window is clipped exactly, so a large facet with its corners outside the window is checked too;\n- each `LatheClearance` refusal, on the bodies of the whole stack (the size cap and `MAX_TIP_GAP`).\n\n## Readings\n\n- CNMG120408 in PCLNR2525M12 (l1 = 150 mm, f1 = 32 mm): orientation 2 (derived), nose radius 0.8 mm, approach 95 degrees, insert angle 80 degrees, clearance 0 degrees, IC 12.7 mm. The nose centre is 0.8 mm up the radial and 0.8 mm along the spindle from the CRP. The CRP maps to (-0.8, 0, -0.8).\n- Boring bar (25 mm, 200 mm long) in a VDI40 sleeve, orientation 3 stated: nose centre at (16.2, 0, -260.2) in the root frame. The bands end at z = 260.2 mm (the sleeve is in them). The sleeve alone gives `TipAway`. The stack passes.\n\n## Pinning tests (`crates/tools/src/lathe_assembly_tests.rs`, in the tools lib)\n\n- `a_cnmg_in_a_pclnr_has_its_nose_centre_at_the_tip`\n- `the_derived_values_equal_todays_tool_record_and_placed_mesh_body` (equivalence pin: the same holder placed by hand on the current mesh path gives the same bands within 1e-9; the nose radius and the orientation equal a `Tool` of kind Turning with `lathe_orientation()`)\n- `the_clearance_envelope_encloses_the_holder`\n- `the_insert_plate_is_left_out_of_the_bands`\n- `hand_and_shape_give_the_orientation_and_a_stated_one_wins` (PCLNL = 1, SRDCN + RCMT = 6)\n- `a_boring_bar_in_a_sleeve_is_judged_as_one_body` (also a bar turned 180 degrees by `z_rot_deg`: refused)\n- `a_mismatch_a_wrong_hand_and_degenerate_input_are_refused_by_name`\n\n## Mutations (each one ran on winbuild, each one is red)\n\n| Mutation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| the box body shrinks in X | RED: `the_clearance_envelope_encloses_the_holder` |\n| the nose-centre shift has the wrong sign | RED: 5 tests |\n| the edge check is off | RED: the wrong-hand test and the 180-degree bar test |\n| the insert plate goes into the bands | RED: `the_insert_plate_is_left_out_of_the_bands` |\n\n## Gate (Windows build lane, winbuild)\n\n- Log `C:\\camcraft-logs\\gate-3643b-27f9f0da32829b9066b6178eb56b7bfd5d484f1a.log`, first line `head 27f9f0da32829b9066b6178eb56b7bfd5d484f1a`. The trial merge with `origin/main` (`e6849c5ae`) was a no-op.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`: 2 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled (`tools suites: lib` ok; `app suites: turning_insert_zone` ok, because `lathe_insert_zone.rs` changed).\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-tools --lib -- lathe_assembly tool_assembly`: 18 passed, 0 failed (the 11 spindle tests stay green after the `placed` refactor).\n- The light lane did not run: only the tools lib changed. P6: no heavy step.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No caller in `crates/app` reads the lathe resolver yet. The turning laws still take their body from the current mesh path.\n- Boring bar overhang in a sleeve (`projection_mm` on a bar) is not in this slice. A bar sits on the port of its sleeve.\n- The approach angle is reported, but it does not change the orientation.\n\n## Still open on #3643\n\n- STEP, STL and OBJ mesh bodies from files (the `LatheBody::mesh` input is ready for them).\n- `MCS_INSERT` as `CsysRole::Mounting` in `crates/app/src/geometry/import/step_csys.rs`.\n- `crates/app/src/tool_assembly.rs` (the app side, with #3644).\n\nChangelog: Tool assemblies: a lathe holder or boring bar with its insert resolves to the nose-centre tip, the orientation (1-8), the nose radius and one clearance body for the whole stack, with named refusals.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nRefs #3643\n",
